==================================================
Lab 4: File Path Traversal, Traversal Sequences Stripped with Superfluous URL-Decode
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website loads product images through a URL that includes the filename.
The server blocks input that contains ../, and then URL-decodes the filename before using it.
Because the decoding happens after the check, an encoded traversal sequence gets past the filter and becomes a real ../ afterward.

Goal: read the contents of /etc/passwd.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When a product image loads, the browser requests /image?filename=<name>.
The server reads the file from its image folder, /var/www/images/, and returns it.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The server checks the filename for ../ before decoding it, then decodes it and uses the result.
The check sees only the encoded text, so it finds nothing to block. The decoding step then turns the encoded characters into a working traversal sequence.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

URL encoding: A way to write special characters in a URL. / becomes %2f, and % itself becomes %25.
Double encoding: Encoding a value that is already encoded. ../ becomes %2e%2e%2f, and encoding the % again gives %252f.
Superfluous decode: An extra decoding step the server performs. Each decode removes one layer of encoding, so the server reveals the traversal sequence only after the filter has already run.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

Instead of a plain traversal sequence, we send:

..%252f..%252f..%252fetc/passwd

The server's filter sees ..%252f, which contains no literal ../, so it lets the value through.
The server then decodes it once, turning %252f into %2f. The value is now ..%2f..%2f..%2fetc/passwd.
The server decodes it again, turning %2f into /. The value is now ../../../etc/passwd, which reads the file.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and load any product image.
Replace the filename in the image URL with ..%252f..%252f..%252fetc/passwd.
Load the page. The text of /etc/passwd appears, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL and builds the full URL with the double-encoded payload already in it.
* It sends a GET request to that URL. The payload is not passed through params, so requests does not encode the % signs again.
* If the server returns status 200, it prints the response text, which is the contents of /etc/passwd.
* If the request fails or the status is different, it prints an error instead.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The filter and the file lookup see the filename at different stages of decoding.
The check runs before the decode, so it never sees the traversal sequence that the lookup eventually uses.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not build file paths from user input. Map each image to an ID or a fixed name on the server.
If user input must be used, decode it fully first, then run the check on the decoded value, and resolve the final path before reading it.
Reject input that still contains encoded characters after decoding, instead of decoding it again.

Other good practices:

Run the web server with only the file permissions it needs.
Keep sensitive files outside the paths the application can reach.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A check that runs before decoding can miss a traversal sequence that appears after decoding.
Validate the value that will actually be used, not an earlier form of it.
Never trust a filename that comes from the user.
